  "editorReview": {
    "text": "The Tide Clerk offers a refreshing twist on the procedural, using the unforgiving logic of the tides as a detective tool; the forensic deductions are genuinely clever and fairly clued. Evelyn Marsh is a compelling protagonist, and the harbour atmosphere is vividly rendered—you can almost taste the bitter tea and smell the damp wool. However, the book occasionally undermines its own rigor. A jarring continuity lapse in Chapter 12 disrupts the tension, and the middle chapters suffer from redundancy, retracing steps rather than advancing. While the prose leans on mechanical patterns and overused",
    "penName": "疏影",
    "title": "Fiction Editor",
    "rating": "Worth reading"
  },
